{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This month is to you the beginning of months.</b> It did not say, 'Speak to all the congregation of the children of Israel: This month is to you,' for that would have implied that the commandment devolves upon every individual of Israel to fix months and to intercalate years. But the commandment is only upon a qualified court, such as Moses and Aaron; therefore the matter came to both of them. Hence it says לכם — 'to you' — meaning a qualified court like yourselves. And it says החדש הזה — 'this month' — which is the month of Nisan, and Scripture calls it first, for it says ראשון הוא לכם, and likewise it is written בראשון בארבעה עשר. And because Tishrei is Rosh HaShanah for years, therefore Scripture says here ראשון הוא לכם — meaning, it is not the head of the year for years, but 'for you' — that is, for your redemption. Therefore Scripture calls the month of Tishrei the seventh, as it says (Numbers 29:1) בחדש השביעי באחד לחדש, and the intent of 'seventh' is relative to the month of Nisan, so that the miracle of the redemption from Egypt be remembered. And for this reason we do not find in the holy tongue specific names for the months of the year, but Scripture says בחדש השני, בחדש השלישי, בחדש השביעי, and so on through all the months of the year, in order that there be through this a remembrance of the redemption from Egypt, which was in Nisan. And what we do find — names for the months of the year, namely Nisan, Iyyar, Sivan, Tammuz, Av, and so on for all of them — these are names of months that came up from Babylon. And the reason for the matter is that at first the counting was a remembrance of the exodus from Egypt, but when we came up from Babylon in the time of the Second Temple, the verse was fulfilled (Jeremiah 16:14–15): ״ולא יאמר עוד חי ה' אשר העלה את ישראל מארץ מצרים כי אם חי ה' אשר העלה ואשר הביא את בני ישראל מארץ צפון״; therefore they needed to call the months by the names by which they were called in Babylon, and this too is for a remembrance, for there we stood in exile and from there the Holy One, blessed be He, redeemed us. And we learn from all this that the months of the year have no specific names in the Torah, just as the days of the week have no specific names, unlike the custom of the nations, who call each and every day by a known name. But we have no specific name for any one of them, and we call each and every one after the Sabbath, and we count אחד בשבת, שני בשבת, and so on for all of them, in order that the remembrance of the Sabbath be present in each and every day. And this is the plain sense of the verse (Exodus 20:8) זכור את יום השבת לקדשו." "<b>And the matter of</b> 'This month is to you the beginning of months': the intent of Scripture is not to warn us about intercalating years and fixing months according to the sighting of the moon. For in the fixing of months there is no fundamental requirement in the Torah to be concerned with the sighting of the moon — if it is seen, well and good, and if it is not seen on the day of fixing but rather earlier or later by a day or two, we are not concerned, for we were not commanded in the Torah to fix the months according to the sighting of the moon but rather according to the calculation." "<b>And our Master Hananel, of blessed memory, wrote</b> that the fixing of the months is only according to the calculation, not according to the sighting of the moon. And the proof is that all the forty years that Israel were in the wilderness, the cloud covered them by day and the pillar of fire by night, and they did not see the sun by day or the moon by night in all of them. And this is what Scripture says (Nehemiah 9): ״ואתה ברחמיך הרבים לא עזבתם במדבר את עמוד הענן לא סר מעליהם ביומם להנחותם בהדרך ואת עמוד האש בלילה להאיר להם״ — and from where would they fix months according to the sighting of the moon? Rather, certainly the fundamental commandment in Scripture is according to the calculation." "<b>And behold,</b> it is a tradition in our hands: the twelve months of the year — five of them, each thirty days; and five of them, each twenty-nine days; and two of them — sometimes both are thirty days, and sometimes they are twenty-nine and twenty-nine, and sometimes one of them is thirty and the second twenty-nine. And these two months are Marcheshvan and Kislev. And it is a tradition in our hands that Rosh Chodesh Tishrei is Rosh HaShanah for years, and that for every month there is a halachah given to Moses from Sinai: twenty-nine days and a half, and 793 parts. And it is explicitly found in Scripture (I Chronicles 12): ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים לדעת מה יעשה ישראל ראשיהם מאתים וכל אחיהם על פיהם״ — and there is nothing that requires wisdom and understanding for fixing times and appointed seasons other than the calculation, which is the secret of the intercalation. But the sighting of the moon is a well-known thing, for when the moon appears everyone knows that it is Rosh Chodesh, and the festival of matzot is on the fifteenth, and Rosh HaShanah on the first of Tishrei, and Yom HaKippurim on the tenth of it, and Sukkot on the fifteenth of it." "<b>But</b> the calculation is only for the wise — they calculate and inform Israel, and they act according to them. And this is what is written: לדעת מה יעשה ישראל — 'to know what Israel should do' — ראשיהם מאתים וכל אחיהם על פיהם — 'their chiefs, two hundred, and all their brethren at their command' — they go out at their word and come in at their word." "<b>And likewise</b> you find from the words of David to Jonathan, who said (I Samuel 20): ״הנה חדש מחר ואנכי ישב אשב עם המלך״ — and from where would David know that tomorrow would be the new moon, were it not that they would fix it according to the calculation? For perhaps the moon would not be seen and they would not fix tomorrow as the new moon. And not only that, but the matter proves that they would observe two days of Rosh Chodesh, as it says (there): ״ויסתר דוד בשדה ויהי החדש וישב המלך על הלחם לאכול ולא דבר שאול מאומה ביום ההוא כי אמר מקרה הוא וגו׳״, and it is written: ״ויהי ממחרת החדש השני ויפקד מקום דוד״ — and it is not reasonable to say that it was a different Rosh Chodesh; therefore it is called החדש השני, as it says (there): ״ויאמר שאול אל יהונתן בנו מדוע לא בא בן ישי גם תמול גם היום אל הלחם ויקם יהונתן מעם השלחן בחרי אף ולא אכל ביום החדש השני לחם״. This proves that in that month there were two days of Rosh Chodesh, as is our custom to this day. When the month was full, all Israel would follow this practice of fixing the months by calculation for eleven hundred years from the days of Moses our teacher until Antigonus, the Exilarch and head of the Sanhedrin. And among his disciples were two, and they are Tzadok and Baitos, and when he expounded to them: 'Do not be like servants who serve the master on condition of receiving reward,' a doubt entered their hearts, and they thought that there is no punishment and no reward, and they went astray, and they began to stir up controversy over the fixing of the moon, saying that the essence of the commandment is not to fix the months by calculation but rather by sighting the moon, and that this is the correct and precise thing. And the sages of the generation needed to refute their words and to inform them with absolute proofs. And thus our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Rabban Gamliel said: Do not be concerned about the sighting of the moon; the calculation is the essence. Thus I have received from the house of my father's father: the new moon is never less than 29 days, 12 hours, and 793 parts." "<b>And behold,</b> these words of his prove that Rabban Gamliel did not rely upon the sighting of the moon but only upon the calculation. And furthermore, our Mishnah is: they saw it — the court and all Israel — and they investigated the witnesses, and they did not have time to say 'sanctified' before night fell — it is intercalated; this is a great proof that they did not hold the essence to be the sighting of the moon, for if the sighting were the essence and they would sanctify by the sighting, how could they intercalate it after the sighting? And greater than this: two came and said: 'We saw it in its proper time, which is the light of the thirtieth, and on the night of its intercalation, which is the eve of the thirtieth day, it was not seen,' and Rabban Gamliel accepted them. And if Rabban Gamliel had relied upon the sighting of the moon and not upon the calculation, it would surely have been established that since it was not seen on the night of its intercalation, they are false witnesses — but rather, surely Rabban Gamliel relied upon the calculation and not upon the testimony of the witnesses. And what you find from our Rabbis of blessed memory, that Rabban Gamliel had diagrams of lunar shapes on a tablet in the wall with which he would show the laymen — the intention in this is that Rabban Gamliel made these diagrams to explain to the disciples of Tzadok and Baitos his knowledge of the course of the moon in each and every month, and that he knew in each and every month in what shape it would be born, whether long or short, and how high it would be in the sphere, and toward which side it would incline. And in each and every month he would explain to the disciples and say to them: 'The moon this month — its shape is such and such, and it inclines toward such and such a side'; and according to that shape which he would show his disciples, by it the witnesses would testify in each and every month. And when they saw thus month after month, year after year, it became clear to them that the essence is nothing but the calculation, and the words of the dissenters were nullified. And even though they would accept the witnesses as a remembrance of the matter, the reliance of the court was only upon the calculation. And according to all, the law of the intercalation is a statute, a law given to Moses from Sinai, that the authority is in the hand of the Great Court when it is supreme, such that there is none above it in wisdom and in fear of sin and in expertise in the chambers of Torah and in the fixing of years — the authority is in its hand to fix and to intercalate according to what it sees fit from the laws of the secret of intercalation. And this is the law that the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded Moses: ״החדש הזה לכם״. End of the commentary of Rabbeinu Chananel of blessed memory. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded this verse: ״החדש הזה לכם״ — the first of the months. Were Israel not to merit any other commandment but this one, that they receive the face of the Shekhinah once a month, it would suffice; here it is written ״החדש הזה״ and there it is written (Exodus 15) ״זה אלי ואנוהו״." "<b>And the matter</b> of this Midrash is an allusion to Knesset Israel, which is called the Crown, sanctified and uniquely designated for Israel, who are עמוסי בטן (laden with womb). Therefore they established in the blessing of the moon: 'a crown of glory for those laden with womb.' And because one who blesses the blessing of the moon receives the face of the Shekhinah, therefore they established that one blesses it standing and not sitting, because of the awe of the Heavenly Kingdom." "<b>And it is known</b> that when a person sees the moon in its renewal and blesses it, he testifies to the renewal of the world, which is the essence of faith, and acknowledges the divinity of the Creator who renewed the world just as He renews the moon in each and every month. And they said that the darkish hue found in it is related to its mourning over Israel, who are likened to the moon when they dwell in exile. And of this Solomon said (Song 6): ״יפה כלבנה ברה כחמה״ — he gave beauty to the moon and brilliance to the sun, because the moon is patterned after the woman in the lower creation, who is affected by the male; so too the moon receives and is affected by the sun. And likewise, 'a woman is only for beauty'; but regarding the sun he mentioned ברה (bright), for its light is clear. And from the fact that he mentioned regarding the sun ברה, you may infer that the moon is not ברה (bright). And do not wonder at this, for even the Shekhinah, which is the lunar force — it too dwells with them in exile and suffers in their distress, as it says (Isaiah 63): ״בכל צרתם לו צר״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״החדש הזה״ — before the Holy One, blessed be He, brought Israel out of Egypt, He informed them that the kingdom would endure for them only thirty generations, as it says: ״החדש הזה לכם״ — just as the moon has thirty days, so their kingdom would be thirty generations. And I have already cited this Midrash at the verse ״ויקרא שמו זרח״." "<b>And further.</b> In the Midrash: החדש הזה לכם — 'this renewal is yours'; בעולם הזה החדש הזה לכם — 'in this world, this renewal is yours.' And the explanation of the matter: 'yours' means that you shall reckon by it, for Israel reckons by the moon, and the moon was given over to Israel to reckon by it — to tell you: just as the moon is visible by day and by night, so Israel in this world and in the world to come. And the seed of Esav reckons by the sun, which is the greater light, and the greater light shall be for the small one — this is Esav, whom the Holy One, blessed be He, called 'small,' as it is said (Jeremiah 49:15): הנה קטן נתתיך בגוים; and the lesser light for the great one — this is Israel, whom the Holy One, blessed be He, called 'great,' as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:8): ומי גוי גדול וגו'; and the seed of those who reckon by the sun are destined to be judged by it, as it is said (Malachi 3:19): ולהט אותם היום הבא. A parable: to a king who betrothed a woman and wrote her few gifts; when he came to marry her, he wrote her many gifts. So too in this world the Holy One, blessed be He, betrothed Israel, as it is written (Hosea 2:21): וארשתיך לי לעולם, and He gave her only the moon, as it is said: החדש הזה לכם; but in the days of the Messiah it shall be the marriage, as it is said (Isaiah 54:5): כי בועליך עושיך ה' צבאות שמו, and at that hour the Holy One, blessed be He, delivers everything to them, as it is said (Daniel 12:3): והמשכילים יזהירו כזוהר הרקיע. <br><b>And we find in Masekhet Soferim</b> and in the Yerushalmi likewise, that one does not recite the blessing over the moon except on the eve of Sabbath, when one is perfumed and in fine garments, and one straightens one's feet toward it and dances before it three times, and blesses: ברוך אשר במאמרו ברא שחקים וגו', and says: ברוך בוראך ברוך יוצרך ברוך מקדשך — 'just as I dance toward you and do not touch you, so if others dance and stand against me, they shall not touch me; may terror fall upon them,' וגו'."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:48.712596215Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}